;; talk/trader--canon.scm — objections to surfaces/trader.scm from the lens of canon.scm.

(objection 'o-trader-canon-1 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'trader-leave-flow :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'no-real-funds-before-dispute-fix "Leaving is the trader's only remedy against a silent hub: d-trader-silent-hub rests on it, and the flow signs exit-prepare, exit-start and exit-finish. The release queue says no real funds until the dispute-replay contract fix lands, and the surface never mentions it. It quietly assumes the fix will land, so a trader with real money on a chain where the old contract is deployed is told that leaving gets their secured share back when the contract can be replayed against them. This needs a maintainer decision (the owner decision on the contract fix); no decision in this file or in kernel.scm holds it, and k-real-or-test lists only the watching service and routed payments as protections that differ per chain."))

(objection 'o-trader-canon-2 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'trader-move-flow :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'htlc-constants-are-simnet "The job is to move money between hubs in minutes, and the flow signs pay-routed with a quoted-fee cap and no chain condition. The queue forbids a real-funds routed payment on an EVM mainnet until per-jurisdiction expiry values are decided, so on that chain the fast path is not offered and the trader is left with the two-chain-wait path they said they cannot use. d-trader-move-money already names this and puts it to the maintainer, so the maintainer decision is recorded, but the flow, the job wording and trader-default-move-cap read as if routed moves work everywhere, and the flow does not use k-real-or-test to refuse real money where it is not allowed."))

(objection 'o-trader-canon-3 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'trader-home :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'recovery-coverage-visible "The first screen answers how much the trader could lose at each counterparty, but shows nothing about whether recovery is covered for that account or whether the watching service accepted or failed its copy. The queue lists that account-level coverage view as a gate on public launch. The need trader-needs-own-evidence and k-evidence supply it, but no screen shows it. On native TRON k-evidence itself says not covered, so for a TRON account the figure on this screen overstates what the trader can recover if their device is lost, and nothing next to it says so."))

(objection 'o-trader-canon-4 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'trader-default-silent-after :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'frontend-is-ui-only "The default makes the surface itself stop sending orders to a hub after ten seconds. d-kernel-silence leans to fact-and-sums, where the kernel states the silence and never stops anything, so the stop is enforced by the face. That is a rule enforced in the frontend, where the runtime is meant to own logic, and the trading program in trader-gap-bot reaches the runtime through k-app-door under the standing rules, not through this face's stop, so the same hub can still be sent orders by the program. Whether stopping is a kernel duty is open in d-kernel-silence (its changes-if names this case) and is not settled; the trader surface treats it as settled."))

(objection 'o-trader-canon-5 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'd-trader-cross-chain :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'launch-flows "The leaning carry-here takes cross-swap, and d-trader-what-it-is takes same-J swap through the ticket, but the file never says on which chain: EVM, native TRON, or both. The launch rule asks the spec to say which surface carries each flow on each chain and not to leave one out silently, and TRON differs materially here because the watching service does not submit there. Each flow carried here also needs its own fast scenario and browser end-to-end test in both wallets (launch-flows-need-owning-tests), and the trader surface adds a ticket flow, a cross flow and a move flow beyond the others. The cost of the gap is that a trader on TRON may find the ticket or the cross screen present but unusable, with no line saying so. Assigning flows per chain is a maintainer matter and no decision in this file holds it."))
